<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The one entitlement this app needs, applied by dm/ios/tools/shell_build.sh when it signs the shell ad hoc.

  Without it iOS gives the process about 3,375 MB, which the bigger games fill: Tears of the Kingdom was killed entering the
  overworld with 3,342 MB resident and 33 MB of headroom left, and lowering the render scale did not help, so the limit is the
  guest heap and not the GPU. With it, a 12 GB phone allows 6-8 GB.

  It has to be in the package's signature before it reaches the phone: an unsigned binary has no signature at all, so an
  install tool has nothing to read the entitlement from and the request is simply lost. That is why the build signs the shell
  with this file instead of leaving it unsigned, and why the tools below keep it when they re-sign:
    AltStore Classic 2.2+, Impactor, Xcode with a personal team   keep it
    SideStore with a free Apple ID                                needs a one-off fix to the App ID
    Sideloadly                                                    does not keep it
  Whether it took effect is one line in Documents/log.txt ("boot: memory limit ...").

  Extended virtual addressing is deliberately absent: it only widens a single address-space reservation past 4 GB, and the
  guest block is 5 GB, which the default limit already allows (6 GB succeeds, 7 GB fails, measured on a device).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
